Title: Case Study: Industrial Formulation Process of Solid Dosage Forms (Tablets)
Subtitle: An Analysis of Excipients, Manufacturing Operations, and Stability Challenges
Prepared By: [Aapka Naam]
Slide 2: Selection of Dosage Form & Active Ingredient
Selected Dosage Form: Oral Solid Dosage – Compressed Tablet
Model Drug (API): Paracetamol (Acetaminophen) 500mg
Rationale: Tablets are the most widely manufactured dosage form globally due to high patient compliance, precise dosing, low production cost, and excellent chemical stability compared to liquids.
Slide 3: Essential Excipients Used & Their Roles
Active drugs (APIs) cannot be compressed alone; they need chemical helpers called excipients:
Diluents/Fillers (e.g., Microcrystalline Cellulose, Lactose): Adds necessary bulk volume to make the tablet size manageable.
Binders (e.g., Polyvinylpyrrolidone - PVP, Starch paste): Acts as an adhesive glue to hold powders together into cohesive granules.
Disintegrants (e.g., Croscarmellose Sodium): Helps the tablet swell and break apart rapidly inside the stomach fluid for drug release.
Lubricants (e.g., Magnesium Stearate): Prevents the powder mixture from sticking to the industrial punching machines.
Slide 4: The Core Formulation Process Flow
Industrial tablet making primarily follows the Wet Granulation pathway to ensure uniform mixing:
Sifting & Blending: API and raw excipients are passed through fine meshes to ensure equal particle size.
Granulation: Binder solution is added to the powder blend to form a damp mass (like wet sand).
Drying: The wet mass is dried in a Fluidized Bed Dryer (FBD) until moisture limits drop below 2\%.
Milling & Lubrication: Dried granules are sized evenly, and lubricants are added.
Compression: High-speed rotary press stations punch the granules into solid, compact tablets.
Slide 5: Critical Stability Factors
To pass regulatory checks, a tablet formulation must resist physical and chemical degradation:
Moisture Management: High residual humidity triggers hydrolysis of vulnerable APIs and causes mold growth. Granules must be dried perfectly.
Temperature Control: Excessive heat during drying can degrade thermosensitive active components.
Photostability: Exposure to UV radiation can discolor the compression layout or alter structural chemical compositions.
Mechanical Shock: High friability means tablets will break or crumble inside bottles during shipping friction.
Slide 6: Industrial Manufacturing Challenges & Solutions
Challenge 1: Capping and Lamination (The upper or lower cap of the tablet separates horizontally during or after compression).
Solution: Optimize mechanical pressure profiles, adjust moisture content slightly, or increase the binder concentration.
Challenge 2: Mottling (Unequal distribution of color across the tablet surface).
Solution: Change the drying temperature configuration or switch to an ultra-fine particle colorant matrix.
Challenge 3: Content Uniformity Failures (Some tablets get more drug, some get less).
Solution: Increase wet blending cycles to guarantee an absolutely homogenous dispersion.
Slide 7: Conclusion & Document Attribution
Summary: Successful tablet engineering relies entirely on balancing binder concentration, optimizing compression force, and protecting the product from moisture.
